WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.900
NOT IN can be surprising when the list contains NULL
Conceptually the

00:00:04.925 --> 00:00:08.050
predicate asks whether a value is different from
every item in the

00:00:08.062 --> 00:00:13.088
list If one item is unknown PostgreSQL cannot prove
that an ordinary

00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:17.688
value is different from that unknown value The
result can become UNKNOWN

00:00:17.712 --> 00:00:21.925
instead of true Because WHERE keeps only true rows
that look like

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:28.125
obvious non-matches may disappear Run the example
and inspect the output The

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.775
safer pattern is to keep NULL out of the list and
handle

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.100
missing status explicitly with IS NULL IS NOT NULL
or another NULL-safe

00:00:35.112 --> 00:00:39.275
predicate Make the missing-value policy visible in
the query
